# Class: obo:HMDM_0000076 (anti-neoplastic agent)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref "PMID:27110842") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:HMDM_0000076 "Antineoplastic drugs (ANDs), also known as cytostatic drugs, are a heterogeneous group of chemicals used to control the growth of neoplastic cells. Most ANDs interfere with cell proliferation through DNA destruction, RNA synthesis, or duplication process. Unfortunately, ANDs do not distinguish cancer cells from normal ones, leading to unwanted adverse effects in patients receiving chemotherapy. The International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC) classifies some ANDs as human carcinogens.")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:contributor obo:HMDM_0000076 <https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace obo:HMDM_0000076 "human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:id obo:HMDM_0000076 "HMDM:0000076")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:HMDM_0000076 "anti-neoplastic agent")
SubClassOf(obo:HMDM_0000076 obo:HMDM_0000073)

# Class: obo:HMDM_0000077 (topoisomerase inhibitor)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref "PMID:22108601") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref "PMID:29363591") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:HMDM_0000077 "Topoisomerase inhibitors are chemical compounds that block the action of topoisomerases. Topoisomerase plays key roles in cellular reproduction and DNA organization, as they facilitate the cleavage of single and double stranded DNA to relax supercoils, untangle catenanes, and condense chromosomes in eukaryotic cells. Topoisomerase inhibitors affect these essential cellular processes by preventing topoisomerases from performing DNA strand breaks or prevent the re-ligation step of the topoisomerase mechanism by interacting with topoisomerase-DNA complexes. These topoisomerase-DNA-inhibitor complexes are cytotoxic agents because the un-repaired single and double stranded DNA breaks that they cause can lead to apoptosis and cell death. Due to this ability to induce apoptosis, topoisomerase inhibitors have gained interest as therapeutics against infectious and cancerous cells.")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:contributor obo:HMDM_0000077 <https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace obo:HMDM_0000077 "human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:id obo:HMDM_0000077 "HMDM:0000077")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:HMDM_0000077 "topoisomerase inhibitor")
SubClassOf(obo:HMDM_0000077 obo:HMDM_0000076)

# Class: obo:HMDM_0000078 (Camptothecin Analogues)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref "PMID:21051639") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:HMDM_0000078 "Camptothecin (CPT) is a topoisomerase inhibitor. It was discovered in 1966 during a systematic screening of natural products for anticancer drugs. It was isolated from the bark and stem of Camptotheca acuminata (Camptotheca, Happy tree), a tree native to China used in traditional Chinese medicine. CPT has showed anticancer activity in clinical trials, especially against breast, ovarian, colon, lung, and stomach cancers. To maximize the therapeutic effects, four CPT analogues have been approved and are used as antineoplastic agents.")
AnnotationAssertion(dcterms:contributor obo:HMDM_0000078 <https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace obo:HMDM_0000078 "human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:id obo:HMDM_0000078 "HMDM:0000078")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:HMDM_0000078 "Camptothecin Analogues")
SubClassOf(obo:HMDM_0000078 obo:HMDM_0000077)

